        "pageInfo": "Not Available",
        "abstractText": "Kenaf (Hibiscus cannabinus L.) leaves, a typically discarded agricultural by-product, have garnered attention for their potential as sources of bioactive compounds. However, systematic optimization of extraction conditions and comprehensive evaluations of bioactivities remain limited. This study investigated the influence of ethanol concentration on the extraction efficiency, phytochemical composition, antioxidant activity, anti-inflammatory effects, and skin-regenerative potential of kenaf leaf extracts (KLEs). KLEs were prepared using water and various ethanol concentrations (30–100 %) to establish a green solvent protocol. Among the samples, kenaf leaf 70 % ethanol extract (KLEE 70 %) exhibited the highest total phenolic content (58.19 mg TAE/g) and flavonoid content (94.17 mg QE/g), along with the strongest antioxidant effects in DPPH (44.61 %) and ABTS (69.37 %) assays and notable enzyme inhibition. KLEE 70 % also significantly reduced nitric oxide (5.33 µM), TNF-α (74.33 pg/mL), and IL-6 (1.33 pg/mL) in LPS-stimulated RAW 264.7 macrophages, while enhancing type I procollagen (4.30 ng/µg), ceramide kinase (3692.89 pg/mL), and hyaluronic acid (9044.69 pg/mL) production in human dermal fibroblasts (HDF). Metabolite profiling using UPLC-ESI-QTOF-MS identified 34 phenolic compounds, including quinic acid derivatives and flavonol glycosides. While kaempferitrin emerged as the predominant flavonoid, several additional isomers, such as kaempferol rhamnosyl pentoside and feruloylquinic acid derivatives, were also detected, indicating a more diverse phenolic profile than previously reported. Quantitative analysis confirmed that KLEE 70 % yielded the highest levels of kaempferitrin and chlorogenic acid. To our knowledge, this is the first report to optimize green-solvent extraction of KLEs and comprehensively assess their skin-regenerative effects, extending prior work that focused only on antioxidant activity or extraction yield. Taken together, our study supports the potential of KLEs as a sustainable and effective natural ingredient for cosmeceutical applications.",
